-- chem/pairenergy.hypercubical — the electric energy of a separated charge pair on the graph is independent of how
-- far apart the two charges are. An edge holonomy transfers a unit of charge from one vertex to another
-- (chem/edgehol.hypercubical), leaving +1 at one end of a path, −1 at the other, and every vertex between them
-- neutral. The electric energy is the sum over the vertices of each one's lowest energy in its sector, and that
-- lowest energy depends only on the vertex's charge (chem/sectorcharge.hypercubical): it is ½K₀₀((4/3)·bal-sq(N) −
-- (1/3)N²) with bal-sq(N) the least Σ_e n_e² over labels summing to N. A neutral vertex has bal-sq(0) = 0 and
-- contributes nothing, so the total is bal-sq(+1) + bal-sq(−1) and the interior length drops out: the electric
-- (potential) part carries no confinement. Any dependence on separation is in the kinetic cost of the holonomies
-- along the path (chem/edgehol and the hop amplitudes), not in the electric energy. Checked: the total electric
-- cost of a ±1 pair is the same on paths of length 3, 5, 7 and 9.
import "sectorcharge.hypercubical"

